# InterHub — current workflow

Follow [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md). Start with [SPEC.md](../../SPEC.md) and the
owning `specs/features/` contract. For specification authoring or a completeness
review use [specs/README.md](../../specs/README.md); a new feature can use
[the template](../../specs/templates/feature.md).

- Frontend: React, TypeScript, RTK/RTK Query, CSS Modules, Rspack, Ant Design v6.
- Backend: Kotlin, Spring Boot, PostgreSQL-compatible persistence.
- Realtime: server-authoritative SSE + POST relay; Yjs editor collaboration.
- Keep current behavior in the owning section, with stable R/AC IDs for new
  requirements. Do not append contradictory dated overrides.
- Make one brief completeness/preservation pass before code; resolve material
  intent questions using evidence first, then the user when a real choice remains.
- For executable behavior, test first and verify the missing behavior when
  practical; choose the relevant E2E, PostgreSQL integration or focused test.
- Significant auth/access, invitation, private-data, realtime or migration
  changes need an independent scoped security/reliability review before release.
- No mandatory role pipeline, OpenSpec/BMAD artifacts, PRD, Linear issue,
  model tier, YAML envelope, mutation run or full-suite gate for every change.
- Specialists are for explicitly delegated work or a needed independent review.
  The role files in `agents/roles/` are optional specialist contracts.
- Preserve user authorization, running applications and default-off flags.
- Keep ordinary context to the relevant feature and applicable dependencies;
  reports and `openspec/` are historical/reference material, read on demand.
